  "body": "I need some help remembering why we ended up where we are, @tyoshino, and maybe fixing it if you agree that it's bad.\n\nCurrent design:\n- needsMore() returns true iff queueSize < HWM\n- needsMore() check is made after enqueuing, so it gets passed (previous queue size + size of just-enqueued chunk)\n- streams by default have a HWM of 0\n\nThus:\n- HWM of 0 (default) => needsMore() always returns false => enqueue(chunk) always returns false => backpressure always applied\n- HWM of 1 => needsMore() always returns false (since 1 < 1 is false) => same, backpressure always applied\n- HWM of 2 => needsMore() returns true the first time but false afterward => enqueuing into an empty queue does not apply backpressure.\n\nMy original vision was that by default, if the producer enqueues slower than the consumer reads, backpressure is never applied. This seems like the correct default, right?\n\nTo get there we can make one of a few tweaks:\n- Default HWM = 2. (Seems weird.)\n- Default HWM = 1, change < to <=.\n- Default HWM = 0, change < to <=, and do the needsMore() check _before_ enqueuing.\n\n(You could also do some other stuff, e.g. flip needsMore() into isFull() or something.)\n\nWDYT? And how does it fit with the kind of extensions you mentioned for more fine-grained flow control?\n",
